"## Summary\n\nIsolation follow-up 22: F21-01, the D-098 archive of follow-up 21's root records, and one assembled run on agent-core 49, for review r28 M-1's first live coverage.\n- **Session:** launched 00:32 EST on 2026-11-04 on #452's and #454's roadmap rows, and delivered at 01:40.\n- **Model:** codex `gpt-6-astra`.\n- **Size:** 4 commits and 444 owned files, +551,126/−74,122. Most of it is records: the D-098 archive of 173 files and the new run's records. The largest file is 1.1 MB, and the total is 46.2 MB.\n- **Checks:**\n  - merges cleanly onto current `main`; prepush-scan clean; owned-check passes.\n  - **The orchestrator reran these** on a scratch tree: current `main` (`e08ebb71`, after #457) with devstack 34 and this branch merged in. All pass:\n    - `make -f mk/at-isolation.mk at-isolation-lint`, `at-isolation-test` and `at-isolation-build`;\n    - the committed-evidence tests in `tests/e2e/at/isolation`, `tests/e2e` and `tests/netlab`;\n    - `make -f mk/e2e.mk e2e-lint` and `go vet -tags e2e ./tests/e2e/...`;\n    - `go test ./tests/handover/...`, including handover 43's claims;\n    - `make go-lint`, gofmt and `git diff --check`.\n- **Scope:** one assembled run in the lane's own namespace, with no rerun and no reservation. It waited 23.6 minutes for another suite to finish (by its times, devstack 34's acceptance run), and no overlap was observed in 53 samples. Zero owned Docker objects at the end.\n\n**The orchestrator's reading.**\n- **F21-01, diagnosed from the product** before any change.\n  - For the fixture's 60 s node window, the agent's renewal lead is 30 s with ±3 s jitter. So an ordinary renewal comes 27 to 33 s after the previous request, not at a fixed 30 s (`node/agent/engine.go:1547`, `node/agent/sync.go:151` and 262–294).\n  - Run 21's gaps were 27.9, 10.5, 32.6, 30.0 and 32.0 s. The 10.5 s gap is the generation 30 to 31 change.\n  - The window for the member's deadline was (03:09:40Z, 03:10:10Z]. The last sampled issue was 1.94 s before it opens, and the next issue, from the full lease history, came 80.5 ms after the cutoff. That corrects the orchestrator's estimate of about 2 s on that side.\n  - **So whether a renewal landed in the window was a matter of phase,** as the orchestrator read it. No product defect.\n- **The schedule's fix: a fresh-renewal anchor.**\n  - Before creating the finite grant, the case waits up to 60 s for a newly accepted serving lease with a 60 s window, at most 5 s old.\n  - From that lease's issue time A, it sets the grant's deadline D = floor(A + 55 s), once, before admission. The eligible window (D − 35 s, D − 5 s] is then about A + (20 s, 50 s], and holds the next ordinary renewal at A + 27 to 33 s.\n  - **Unchanged:** the >25 s node extension, the D − 5 s cutoff, the matching signed member and service deadlines, the positive control, the denial predicate, all five probes and the six serving reads. No grant or lease is edited.\n  - **Proofs, offline:** run 21's original times replay into the window under the new schedule and stay blocked under the old one. Synthetic sequences cover the nominal interval, both jitter limits, a one-second step delay, the 10.5 s early renewal and a two-second observation delay. A missing window still blocks, naming its evidence.\n- **The run:** `7b0db5f2` on `0b95e69a`, which is `main` after agent-core 49 plus this lane's schedule. 06:01–06:27Z, 1,578.6 s. **27 pass, 2 blocked, 0 fail;** exit 2.\n  - AT10 9/0/0, AT11 2/1/0, AT12 10/0/0, AT13 6/1/0.\n  - **Blocked:** AT11's IPv6 platform matrix and AT13's full continuous fault matrix, the two operator matrices.\n- **F21-01 is answered, with r28 M-1's first live pass.**\n  - The member's deadline was 06:19:21Z, and the node's accepted lease ran to 06:19:47.33Z, 26.3 s later.\n  - After the deadline, all five member requests timed out and the bytes stayed at 2,631. All six reads showed the node serving, with a non-baseline member chain. The probes finished at 06:19:46.40Z, before the node's lease expired.\n  - Run 21 stays blocked, and run 20's pass stays recorded without counting as serving-node evidence.\n- **The short whole-node expiry** again passes D-140 and qualifies under D-142: 165 positives and 90 drops, closing in (D − 98.8 ms, D − 96.7 ms], with no interleavings.\n- **Node writes:** 15 commands in 16 attempts. One publication PUT got a 412; the re-read returned the newer ETag, and the second PUT, with the same key and body, got 202. Both attempts and the read-back are retained.\n- **Agent restarts:** four restart windows retained. No `baseline_unverified` and no `GENERATION_MISMATCH` in the agent's and the watchdog's logs, so F48-01's signature did not recur. That does not prove a forced D-156 interleaving.\n- **F22-01, new: the included D-120 reload/revoke lab failed.**\n  - `TestAT10_AT13_ReloadRevokeBaseline` clause (iv): one TCP read timed out, at sequence 101.\n  - All 101 Applies verified, with 103 datagrams, no UDP loss and no install over 100 ms.\n  - The failure stays recorded, with no fixture or product edit and no rerun.\n  - **Routed to netlab 35,** which owns the fixture. It diagnoses from this run's retained records first, and brings in agent-core only if the records point at the product.\n- **Carried:** AT11's native platform matrix, AT13's full continuous fault matrix, and F17-01, pure `stale_generation`.\n"
